# Behavioural tests: Tiger programs, compiled by tigerc and qc, run, and
# checked against their expected stdout and exit code.
#
# Translated from tiger.tst, which was Lua read by qc--'s testdrv.lua. That
# driver is gone - qc-- no longer embeds Lua - but the data is worth keeping:
# the file list, which tests need stdin, and what each one should print.
#
# Dropped from the original: the .s/.s1/.s2 assembly comparisons against
# x86/*.s.gz. Those cannot pass now. qc-- uses a different register allocator
# from the 2005 compiler that produced them and runs no optimiser, so its
# output legitimately differs everywhere. The behavioural half - what the
# program prints and what it returns - is the half that says the compiler
# works.
#
# Expected exit codes come from qc--'s own tests/tiger.tests. They are not 0
# and that is not a bug: a Tiger program's value is the value of its body, so
# "print(x)" returns whatever tig_print returns, which is printf's character
# count. hello prints 14 characters and exits 14.
#
# Columns:  name  source  expected-rc  stdin ("-" for none)
# Expected stdout is always x86/<name>.1

arrays      arrays.tig      1    -
colmajor    colmajor.tig    0    misc/colmajor.0
forloop     forloop.tig     1    -
funcall     funcall.tig     1    -
hello       hello.tig       14   -
merge       merge.tig       1    -
qsort       qsort.tig       0    -
queens      queens.tig      0    -
rc4         rc4.tig         215  misc/rc4.0
sieve       sieve.tig       0    -
wf          wf.tig          0    misc/wf.0
